=== phase 4: lifetime ===
py_is_object
  a handle is an object            ok
  a list is not                    ok
  an atom is not                   ok
  an unbound var is not            ok

marshalling borrows
  2000 translations, count drift   ok

a handle owns exactly one reference
  50 handles, count rises by       ok
  after py_free of all 50          ok

py_free of the first handle
  releases one reference           ok

errors
instantiation_error  py_free of an unbound var        
type_error(py_object,foo)  py_free of a non-object          
type_error(py_object,[1,2])  py_free of a list                

the declared reference discipline
  every ptr return is classified   ok
  no pointer return says none      ok
  every ccstr return is borrowed   ok
  nothing else claims a reference  ok
  every steals names a real entry  ok
  45 entry points declared

=== phase 5: errors ===
Python exceptions become Prolog exceptions
  divide by zero               ZeroDivisionError: division by zero
  bad int literal              ValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'notanumber'
  missing attribute            AttributeError: module 'math' has no attribute 'no_such_attr'
  missing dict key             KeyError: 'missing'
  wrong argument type          TypeError: must be real number, not str
  too many arguments           TypeError: math.sqrt() takes exactly one argument (2 given)
  raised from Python code      RuntimeError: deliberate

Prolog faults stay on this side
instantiation_error  unbound goal                 
instantiation_error  unbound argument             
type_error(python_term,foo(bar))  untranslatable argument      
domain_error(py_option,nope(x))  bad option                   
type_error(list,nope)  options not a list           
domain_error(py_constant,maybe)  bad @ constant               
type_error(py_object,hello)  py_free of a non-object      
  unknown module               ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'no_such_module_qq'

the interpreter is still usable afterwards
  a call after an error        ok
  no error left pending        ok
  errors in a loop             ok

the pre-3.12 fetch path gives the same answer
  via PyErr_Fetch              ValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'notanumber'
  api in use                   raised_exception
  back to GetRaisedException   ValueError

